  + then path — the report prints an honest tie-break note)). Day 169: the ranked list is ordered by **`(study tier asc, score desc, current risk score desc, path asc)`** — the tier is the **predicate form** of the two study-history weights, which are unchanged (`W_RECENTLY_STUDIED = -1.0`, `W_VISITED_UNGRADED = -0.5`) and now only order files *within* a group. The reason the weight alone was not enough: this report's real consumer is a **top-N selector**, not a score reader — `scripts/extract_trajectory.py` takes `EPISTEMIC_TOP_N = 3` and hands those three files to the planner as "point your self-driven slot here", and a −1.0 discount cannot move a file scoring +1.0 (never graded) +1.0 (stale) off the top. That is the Day-165 lesson verbatim: a signal added as a **weight** reaches exactly the consumers that compute a weighted score, and any sibling view that **filters or selects** is opted out with no diff and no error, so the signal has to be re-expressed as a predicate in the same diff. It is #744's defect one layer up — that fix stopped the *never-forecast* section re-queuing already-studied files; the ranked half was still doing it. **The measured observation (Day 169, before the change): 8 of the top 10 were files already studied by a graded round, and the only two never-studied entries sat at ranks 9 and 10** — i.e. the exploration budget was aimed at the three most recently lit rooms and the planner never saw the dark ones. After the change the same command leads with the `dark — no deliberate study on record` group and `src/dispatch_sub.rs` / `src/safety.rs` are in the shown slice; a future session can re-run `./target/debug/yoyo risk epistemic` and check whether that still holds. The decision half is the pure, `Ord`-derived `StudyTier { NeverStudied, VisitedUngraded, Graded }` plus `study_tier(Option<&StudyState>) -> StudyTier` (absence is the darkest value and keeps its own name rather than folding into its neighbour); the variant order **is** the fix, so it is pinned by an explicit `NeverStudied < VisitedUngraded < Graded` assertion rather than left to the declaration order. `format_epistemic_report` prints one `study_tier_header` per group, emitted only when the group is non-empty, and the numbering stays contiguous across groups. **Consumer guard (checked, not assumed):** no header contains the substring `never forecast` (which would hard-stop `extract_trajectory.py`'s collection at `EPISTEMIC_NEVER_FORECAST_RE`), the numbered row shape is byte-identical so `EPISTEMIC_ENTRY_RE` still matches, and a test asserts no header line matches that regex; `python3 scripts/extract_trajectory.py --test` passes. The closing note was rewritten because `high score = the model is blindest here` became false the moment a 0.5 dark file outranks a 1.5 studied one — it now states the ordering and owns the judgment call (a studied file ranks below every unstudied one regardless of score; within a group, higher score still means blinder). Weights, the never-forecast section (`commands_risk_neverforecast.rs`) and `EPISTEMIC_TOP_N` were deliberately left untouched. Day 164:
